\section{Introduction}

The hierarchy problem remains one of the most persistent conceptual tensions in
high-energy physics. The enormous disparity between the electroweak scale,
$v \approx 246~\text{GeV}$, and the Planck scale,
$M_{\text{Pl}} \approx 1.22\times 10^{19}~\text{GeV}$, suggests that the Standard
Model (SM) is incomplete. Warped extra-dimensional models, beginning with the
Randall--Sundrum (RS) construction, provide a geometric mechanism for generating
large scale hierarchies without fine-tuning.

In the RS1 model, the five-dimensional spacetime is described by the metric
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = e^{-2k|y|}\eta_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu + dy^2,
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the curvature scale of the AdS$_5$ bulk and $y$ is the coordinate of
the compact extra dimension. The exponential warp factor generates a natural
hierarchy between the ultraviolet (UV) and infrared (IR) branes.

However, the RS framework traditionally assumes a harmonic bulk potential for
scalar fields, leading to a nearly equidistant Kaluza--Klein (KK) spectrum. This
equidistance is a direct consequence of the underlying Sturm--Liouville structure
and is one of the most rigid predictions of RS geometry.

In this work, we introduce the STME-RS-A model, which incorporates a quartic
anharmonic term in the bulk scalar potential:
\begin{equation}
V(\Phi) = \lambda \Phi^2 + \lambda_4 \Phi^4.
\end{equation}
The presence of $\lambda_4$ modifies the eigenvalue problem and produces
non-equidistant KK masses. This deviation is not a small perturbation but a
structural shift in the geometry, providing a clear experimental signature.

We derive the full KK spectrum, analyze the anharmonic corrections, and construct
benchmark points suitable for collider searches. We then compute production
cross sections, decay widths, and exclusion contours for the High-Luminosity LHC
(HL-LHC). Our results demonstrate that the STME-RS-A model is experimentally
falsifiable and provides a unique window into extra-dimensional physics.

