\section{Warped Geometry and Bulk Dynamics}

The STME-RS-A framework is constructed on a slice of AdS$_5$ spacetime with
non-trivial scalar dynamics in the bulk. In this section we review the geometric
structure, derive the Einstein equations, and introduce the anharmonic bulk
potential responsible for the non-equidistant KK spectrum.

\subsection{Metric Structure}

We consider the standard warped metric
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = e^{-2k|y|}\eta_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu + dy^2,
\label{eq:metric}
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the curvature scale and $y$ is the coordinate of the compact
extra dimension. The UV brane is located at $y=0$ and the IR brane at $y=L$.

The warp factor generates an exponential hierarchy:
\begin{equation}
m{\text{IR}} = e^{-kL} m{\text{UV}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Bulk Scalar Potential}

The scalar field $\Phi$ propagates in the bulk with an anharmonic potential
\begin{equation}
V(\Phi) = \lambda \Phi^2 + \lambda_4 \Phi^4.
\label{eq:potential}
\end{equation}
The quartic term $\lambda_4$ introduces a deviation from the harmonic confinement
assumed in RS models. This modification alters the Sturm--Liouville eigenvalue
problem and produces non-equidistant KK masses.

The scalar action is
\begin{equation}
S\Phi = \int d^4x \int0^L dy \sqrt{-g}
\left[
-\frac{1}{2}g^{MN}\partialM\Phi\partialN\Phi - V(\Phi)
\right].
\end{equation}

\subsection{Einstein Equations in the Bulk}

The five-dimensional Einstein equations are
\begin{equation}
G{MN} = \kappa5^2 T_{MN},
\end{equation}
with bulk stress-energy tensor
\begin{equation}
T{MN} = \partialM\Phi\partial_N\Phi
- g_{MN}\left[
\frac{1}{2}g^{AB}\partialA\Phi\partialB\Phi + V(\Phi)
\right].
\end{equation}

The scalar equation of motion is
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{-g}}\partialM\left(\sqrt{-g}g^{MN}\partialN\Phi\right)
= \frac{dV}{d\Phi}.
\label{eq:eom}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Boundary Conditions on the Branes}

The scalar field satisfies boundary conditions determined by brane-localized
potentials:
\begin{align}
\left.\partialy\Phi\right|{y=0} &= \frac{dV_{\text{UV}}}{d\Phi}, \\
\left.\partialy\Phi\right|{y=L} &= -\frac{dV_{\text{IR}}}{d\Phi}.
\end{align}

These conditions determine the allowed KK modes and their masses.

