\section{Sturm--Liouville Reduction and KK Mode Structure}

We derive the full Sturm--Liouville eigenvalue problem governing
the KK tower in the STME-RS-A framework. The presence of the
anharmonic bulk potential modifies both the eigenfunctions and eigenvalues,
leading to non-equidistant KK masses.

\subsection{Eigenvalue Problem}

We expand the bulk scalar field $\Phi(x,y)$ in KK modes:
\begin{equation}
\Phi(x,y) = \sum{n=0}^{\infty} \phin(x) f_n(y),
\end{equation}
where $f_n(y)$ are eigenfunctions of the Sturm--Liouville operator
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}[f_n] = -\frac{1}{e^{-4k|y|}} \frac{d}{dy}
\left( e^{-4k|y|} \frac{df_n}{dy} \right)
+ \frac{d^2 V}{d\Phi^2} f_n.
\end{equation}

The eigenvalue equation is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}[fn] = mn^2 f_n,
\label{eq:SL}
\end{equation}
with orthonormality condition
\begin{equation}
\int0^L dy\, e^{-2k|y|} fn(y) fm(y) = \delta{nm}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Harmonic vs Anharmonic Contributions}

For the harmonic RS case ($\lambda_4 = 0$), the effective potential is quadratic:
\begin{equation}
V_{\text{harm}}''(\Phi) = 2\lambda.
\end{equation}
In the anharmonic case,
\begin{equation}
V{\text{anharm}}''(\Phi) = 2\lambda + 12\lambda4 \Phi^2.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Analytic Approximations}

To first order in $\lambda_4$, the mass shift is
\begin{equation}
\Delta m_n^2 \approx
\frac{12\lambda_4}{16\lambda^2}
\left( 6n^2 + 6n + 3 \right).
\label{eq:mass_shift}
\end{equation}
Thus the full mass spectrum is
\begin{equation}
m_n^2 =
4k^2
+ 2\sqrt{\lambda}\left(n+\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
+ \Delta m_n^2.
\end{equation}

