\section{Conclusions}

The STME-RS-A framework introduces a controlled anharmonic deformation of the
Randall--Sundrum geometry, producing a non-equidistant KK mass spectrum with
experimentally resolvable signatures. The quartic bulk term $\lambda_4$ modifies
the Sturm--Liouville eigenvalue problem, shifts KK masses, and enhances IR
localization of wavefunctions, thereby altering couplings and branching fractions.

We have derived the full KK spectrum, computed analytic and numerical mass
eigenvalues, and constructed benchmark points BP1--BP4 suitable for collider
searches. Collider analyses show that the HL-LHC can discover the first KK mode
for all benchmark points and can exclude the first two modes across the parameter
space, while FCC-hh can probe the full KK tower.

